/* D&D 2024 Basic Rules - the 12 classes: proficiencies, per-level numbers, every class feature, subclass, multiclassing.
   levels[]: lvl, pb (proficiency bonus), features (names), attacks (attacks per Attack action), class-specific numbers
   (rages, rageDamage, weaponMastery, martialArtsDie, sneakAttack, actionSurgeUses, indomitableUses, metamagic (options known),
   arcanum (Mystic Arcanum spell levels), spellbook (spells in a Wizard's book) ...) and spells {cantrips, prepared, slots[1-9]}.
   features[]: { lvl, name, text, choice?, grants? } - every class feature in level order (subclass.features the same).
     choice = what the player picks when gaining it: { type: 'subclass' | 'feat' (category: general / epic-boon / fighting-style /
       origin; 'or' = an alternative such as Blessed Warrior) | 'skills' (count, from: 'class' | 'any') | 'expertise' (count, from?,
       languages?) | 'languages' | 'weaponMastery' (count from the levels table) | 'invocations' (count from the table) |
       'metamagic' (count) | 'spell' (level, list) | 'spells' (count, lists / levels / from) | 'spellbook' (count) |
       'option' (options: [{ name, text, grants? }], swappable?) }
     grants = what changes on the sheet: { speed, saves, abilityScores + max, hpPerClassLevel, weapons, armor, languages,
       alwaysPrepared: [spell names], cantrips, jackOfAllTrades }
   toolChoice: { count, from } (tools picked at level 1). multiclass: { requires: abilities needing 13+ (requiresAny: one of them is enough), hitDie, weapons, armor, skills, tools, text }
   subclass: { name, features, attackFeatures, spells: { classLevel: [always-prepared spells] } (Druid: by land type) }
   warlock.invocations / sorcerer.metamagic / sorcerer.fontOfMagic ([slot level, point cost, min Sorcerer level]) /
   druid.wildShapeForms ([Druid level, known forms, max CR, fly]) / wizard.spellbook ({ start, perLevel }).
   Source: D&D Beyond Basic Rules (2024) / SRD 5.2.1 (CC-BY-4.0, Wizards of the Coast). Numbers and names come from the
   rules data; feature text is condensed into short plain-language summaries (not the book text). */
